# Which PYNQ board this checkout drives.  Copy to board.conf and edit:
#
#     cp board.conf.example board.conf
#
# board.conf is NOT tracked by git and never leaves your machine.  env.sh sources it when
# PYNQ_HOST is not already set in the environment, so an explicit
#
#     PYNQ_HOST=xilinx@10.0.0.42 scripts/20_rocket_run.sh ...
#
# still wins over anything written here.
#
# WHY THERE IS NO DEFAULT.  env.sh used to default PYNQ_HOST to the address of one
# particular board.  That is convenient on exactly one machine and wrong everywhere
# else: on a shared segment it is somebody else's board, and a lab that loads a bitstream
# into a stranger's PL is worse than one that refuses to start.  Scripts that need a board
# now fail with an instruction instead.

# The board, as ssh addresses it.  user@host.  The stock PYNQ v3.1.1 image logs in as
# `xilinx`, and every stock card brings up a static alias on 192.168.2.99, so a board wired
# point-to-point to this machine is usually reachable there.  On a shared network use the
# address your DHCP server actually gave it -- see fpga/pynq-z2/docs/BRINGUP.md.
#PYNQ_HOST=xilinx@<board-ip>

# Staging directory ON THE BOARD.  Scripts push zephyr.bin, a bitstream and a runner here and
# pull console.out / tacit.out back.  Nothing authoritative lives on a board; this is scratch.
# The default (/home/xilinx/tutorial) is right on any stock image.
#PYNQ_DIR=/home/xilinx/tutorial

# A SHORT LOGICAL NAME for this board, stamped into results.csv and run.json so a row can
# never be blended with another machine's.  fpga/pynq-z2/bwlab/boards.csv is a per-bench
# register and ships EMPTY: either add a row for your board, or just name it here and
# scripts/lib/board_id.sh stops asking.
#IISWC_BOARD=my-pynq
